  1→---
  2→title: Working with Maps
  3→description: Learn how to create and manipulate map containers in Automerge documents
  4→---
  5→
  6→# Overview
  7→
  8→Maps store key-value pairs, similar to dictionaries or JSON objects.
  9→
 10→Every Automerge document starts with a root map. You can create nested maps to build complex hierarchical data structures.
 11→
 12→# Creating Maps
 13→
 14→## Root Map
 15→
 16→Every document has a root map automatically:
 17→
 18→```csharp
 19→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 20→var root = doc.RootId;
 21→
 22→doc.PutString(root, "name", "Alice");
 23→doc.Commit("Add name");
 24→```
 25→
 26→## Nested Maps
 27→
 28→Create nested maps using `PutObject`:
 29→
 30→```csharp
 31→var root = doc.RootId;
 32→
 33→// Create a nested map for user preferences
 34→var preferences = doc.PutObject(root, "preferences", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
 35→doc.PutString(preferences, "theme", "dark");
 36→doc.PutBoolean(preferences, "notifications", true);
 37→
 38→doc.Commit("Add preferences");
 39→```
 40→
 41→This creates a structure like:
 42→
 43→```json
 44→{
 45→  "preferences": {
 46→    "theme": "dark",
 47→    "notifications": true
 48→  }
 49→}
 50→```
 51→
 52→# Adding Values
 53→
 54→Automerge provides type-specific methods for adding different data types to maps.
 55→
 56→## Strings
 57→
 58→Store text values:
 59→
 60→```csharp
 61→doc.PutString(root, "username", "alice");
 62→doc.PutString(root, "email", "alice@example.com");
 63→```
 64→
 65→Keys must be strings. Values are UTF-8 encoded text.
 66→
 67→## Numbers
 68→
 69→Store integer values:
 70→
 71→```csharp
 72→doc.PutInt64(root, "age", 30);
 73→doc.PutInt64(root, "score", -42);
 74→doc.PutUInt64(root, "points", 1000);
 75→```
 76→
 77→Use `PutInt64` for signed integers and `PutUInt64` for unsigned integers.
 78→
 79→Store floating-point values:
 80→
 81→```csharp
 82→doc.PutDouble(root, "temperature", 23.5);
 83→doc.PutDouble(root, "rating", 4.7);
 84→```
 85→
 86→## Booleans
 87→
 88→Store true or false values:
 89→
 90→```csharp
 91→doc.PutBoolean(root, "is_active", true);
 92→doc.PutBoolean(root, "email_verified", false);
 93→```
 94→
 95→## Timestamps
 96→
 97→Store dates and times as Unix milliseconds:
 98→
 99→```csharp
100→var now = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds();
101→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "created_at", now);
102→
103→var future = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddDays(7).ToUnixTimeMilliseconds();
104→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "expires_at", future);
105→```
106→
107→Timestamps use Int64 internally but have semantic meaning for time values.
108→
109→## Binary Data
110→
111→Store arbitrary bytes:
112→
113→```csharp
114→var imageData = File.ReadAllBytes("avatar.png");
115→doc.PutBytes(root, "avatar", imageData);
116→
117→var hash = new byte[] { 0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, 0xEF };
118→doc.PutBytes(root, "checksum", hash);
119→```
120→
121→This works for any binary data like images, encrypted data, or serialized objects.
122→
123→## Null Values
124→
125→Store explicit null:
126→
127→```csharp
128→doc.PutNull(root, "deleted_at");
129→```
130→
131→Null differs from a missing key. This explicitly sets the key to null.
132→
133→## Counters
134→
135→Counters are special CRDT values that merge by addition:
136→
137→```csharp
138→doc.PutCounter(root, "view_count", 10);
139→doc.Commit("Initial count");
140→
141→// Later, increment by 5
142→doc.PutCounter(root, "view_count", 5);
143→doc.Commit("Add more views");
144→```
145→
146→When documents merge, counter values add together. If Alice increments by 10 and Bob increments by 5 concurrently, the merged value becomes 15.
147→
148→This differs from regular integers, which use last-write-wins semantics.
149→
150→## Nested Containers
151→
152→Create nested maps, lists, or text containers:
153→
154→```csharp
155→var root = doc.RootId;
156→
157→// Nested map
158→var address = doc.PutObject(root, "address", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
159→doc.PutString(address, "city", "San Francisco");
160→doc.PutString(address, "country", "USA");
161→
162→// Nested list
163→var tags = doc.PutObject(root, "tags", AutomergeContainerType.List);
164→doc.ListInsertString(tags, 0, "golang");
165→doc.ListInsertString(tags, 1, "rust");
166→
167→// Nested text
168→var bio = doc.PutObject(root, "bio", AutomergeContainerType.Text);
169→doc.SpliceText(bio, 0, 0, "Software engineer interested in distributed systems");
170→
171→doc.Commit("Add nested structures");
172→```
173→
174→# Updating Values
175→
176→To update a value, use the same `Put` methods:
177→
178→```csharp
179→// Initial value
180→doc.PutString(root, "status", "draft");
181→doc.Commit("Set initial status");
182→
183→// Update the value
184→doc.PutString(root, "status", "published");
185→doc.Commit("Publish document");
186→```
187→
188→Updates create new versions. The change history tracks both values.
189→
190→# Deleting Keys
191→
192→Remove keys from maps using `Delete`:
193→
194→```csharp
195→doc.PutString(root, "temp_field", "temporary");
196→doc.Commit("Add temp field");
197→
198→doc.Delete(root, "temp_field");
199→doc.Commit("Remove temp field");
200→```
201→
202→After deletion, the key no longer appears in the map. However, the history still contains the previous value.
203→
204→# Reading Values
205→
206→Use `HydrateJson` to get a JSON representation:
207→
208→```csharp
209→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
210→Console.WriteLine(json);
211→```
212→
213→This returns a string you can parse with `System.Text.Json` or `Newtonsoft.Json`:
214→
215→```csharp
216→using System.Text.Json;
217→
218→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
219→using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
220→var root = parsed.RootElement;
221→
222→if (root.TryGetProperty("username", out var username))
223→{
224→    Console.WriteLine($"Username: {username.GetString()}");
225→}
226→```
227→
228→## Getting Keys
229→
230→Retrieve all keys in a map:
231→
232→```csharp
233→var root = doc.RootId;
234→doc.PutString(root, "name", "Alice");
235→doc.PutInt64(root, "age", 30);
236→doc.Commit("Add data");
237→
238→var keys = doc.GetKeys(root);
239→foreach (var key in keys)
240→{
241→    Console.WriteLine($"Key: {key}");
242→}
243→// Output:
244→// Key: name
245→// Key: age
246→```
247→
248→This returns an alphabetically sorted list of keys present in the map.
249→
250→# Complex Example
251→
252→Here's a complete example showing multiple map operations:
253→
254→```csharp
255→using System;
256→using System.Text.Json;
257→using Ray.Toolkit.Automerge;
258→
259→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
260→var root = doc.RootId;
261→
262→// Basic user information
263→doc.PutString(root, "user_id", "usr_12345");
264→doc.PutString(root, "username", "alice");
265→doc.PutString(root, "email", "alice@example.com");
266→doc.PutBoolean(root, "email_verified", true);
267→
268→// Metadata
269→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
270→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "last_login", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
271→
272→// Statistics using counters
273→doc.PutCounter(root, "posts_count", 0);
274→doc.PutCounter(root, "likes_count", 0);
275→
276→// User preferences as nested map
277→var preferences = doc.PutObject(root, "preferences", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
278→doc.PutString(preferences, "language", "en");
279→doc.PutString(preferences, "timezone", "America/Los_Angeles");
280→doc.PutBoolean(preferences, "dark_mode", true);
281→doc.PutBoolean(preferences, "email_notifications", true);
282→
283→// Subscription details as nested map
284→var subscription = doc.PutObject(root, "subscription", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
285→doc.PutString(subscription, "plan", "pro");
286→doc.PutString(subscription, "status", "active");
287→doc.PutTimestamp(subscription, "started_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
288→var expiresAt = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddYears(1).ToUnixTimeMilliseconds();
289→doc.PutTimestamp(subscription, "expires_at", expiresAt);
290→
291→doc.Commit("Create user profile");
292→
293→// Display the structure
294→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
295→using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
296→Console.WriteLine(JsonSerializer.Serialize(parsed.RootElement, new JsonSerializerOptions
297→{
298→    WriteIndented = true
299→}));
300→
301→// Update some values
302→doc.PutString(root, "email", "alice.smith@example.com");
303→doc.PutBoolean(root, "email_verified", false);
304→doc.Commit("Update email address");
305→
306→// Increment counters
307→doc.PutCounter(root, "posts_count", 1);
308→doc.PutCounter(root, "likes_count", 5);
309→doc.Commit("Add post and likes");
310→```
311→
312→# Merge Behavior
313→
314→When two documents modify different keys, both changes survive:
315→
316→```csharp
317→// Alice's document
318→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
319→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
320→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "title", "Project Plan");
321→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "owner", "Alice");
322→alice.Commit("Alice: Add title and owner");
323→
324→// Bob's document
325→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
326→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
327→bob.PutString(bobRoot, "status", "in-progress");
328→bob.PutInt64(bobRoot, "priority", 1);
329→bob.Commit("Bob: Add status and priority");
330→
331→// Merge Bob's changes into Alice's document
332→var bobChanges = bob.GetChanges(new string[0]);
333→alice.ApplyChanges(bobChanges);
334→
335→// Alice's document now has all four keys
336→var json = alice.HydrateJson();
337→// Result contains: title, owner, status, priority
338→```
339→
340→When both modify the same key, last-write-wins applies:
341→
342→```csharp
343→// Both update "status"
344→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "status", "completed");
345→alice.Commit("Alice: Mark complete");
346→
347→bob.PutString(bobRoot, "status", "blocked");
348→bob.Commit("Bob: Mark blocked");
349→
350→// After merge, one value wins based on timestamp
351→// Usually the later timestamp, with actor ID as tiebreaker
352→```
353→
354→# Counter Merge Behavior
355→
356→Counters merge differently than regular values. They add together:
357→
358→```csharp
359→// Alice increments view count by 10
360→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
361→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
362→alice.PutCounter(aliceRoot, "views", 10);
363→alice.Commit("Alice: Add 10 views");
364→
365→// Bob increments view count by 5
366→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
367→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
368→bob.PutCounter(bobRoot, "views", 5);
369→bob.Commit("Bob: Add 5 views");
370→
371→// After merge, views = 15 (10 + 5)
372→var bobChanges = bob.GetChanges(new string[0]);
373→alice.ApplyChanges(bobChanges);
374→
375→var json = alice.HydrateJson();
376→// views: 15
377→```
378→
379→This makes counters useful for analytics, voting systems, and accumulating metrics.
380→
381→# Best Practices
382→
383→## Use Meaningful Keys
384→
385→Choose descriptive key names:
386→
387→```csharp
388→// Good
389→doc.PutString(root, "user_email", "alice@example.com");
390→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "created_at", timestamp);
391→
392→// Unclear
393→doc.PutString(root, "e", "alice@example.com");
394→doc.PutTimestamp(root, "ts", timestamp);
395→```
396→
397→## Group Related Data
398→
399→Use nested maps to group related fields:
400→
401→```csharp
402→// Good: Grouped by concept
403→var address = doc.PutObject(root, "address", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
404→doc.PutString(address, "street", "123 Main St");
405→doc.PutString(address, "city", "San Francisco");
406→doc.PutString(address, "zip", "94102");
407→
408→// Less organized: Flat structure
409→doc.PutString(root, "address_street", "123 Main St");
410→doc.PutString(root, "address_city", "San Francisco");
411→doc.PutString(root, "address_zip", "94102");
412→```
413→
414→## Use Counters for Metrics
415→
416→When multiple actors might update the same numeric value, use counters:
417→
418→```csharp
419→// Good: Counter for concurrent increments
420→doc.PutCounter(root, "view_count", 1);
421→
422→// Problematic: Regular int with concurrent updates
423→doc.PutInt64(root, "view_count", currentValue + 1);
424→```
425→
426→## Batch Related Changes
427→
428→Make related changes together in one commit:
429→
430→```csharp
431→// Good: Related changes in one commit
432→doc.PutString(root, "first_name", "Alice");
433→doc.PutString(root, "last_name", "Smith");
434→doc.PutString(root, "email", "alice.smith@example.com");
435→doc.Commit("Update user name and email");
436→
437→// Less efficient: Many small commits
438→doc.PutString(root, "first_name", "Alice");
439→doc.Commit("Update first name");
440→doc.PutString(root, "last_name", "Smith");
441→doc.Commit("Update last name");
442→```
443→
444→# Next Steps
445→
446→**Working with Lists** - Learn how to use list containers for ordered data.
447→
448→**Working with Text** - Explore collaborative text editing.
449→
450→**Synchronization** - Understand how to sync map changes between peers.
451→
452→**Persistence** - Save and load documents containing maps.
