  1→---
  2→title: Working with Lists
  3→description: Learn how to create and manipulate list containers in Automerge documents
  4→---
  5→
  6→# Overview
  7→
  8→Lists store ordered sequences of items, similar to arrays.
  9→
 10→Lists support insertion at any position, removal of items, and nested containers. They maintain order even when multiple users make concurrent changes.
 11→
 12→# Creating Lists
 13→
 14→Create a list using `PutObject` with the List container type:
 15→
 16→```csharp
 17→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 18→var root = doc.RootId;
 19→
 20→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 21→```
 22→
 23→This creates an empty list stored under the "items" key in the root map.
 24→
 25→# Inserting Items
 26→
 27→Lists use index-based insertion. Index 0 inserts at the beginning, and higher indices insert further along.
 28→
 29→## Strings
 30→
 31→Insert text values:
 32→
 33→```csharp
 34→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "todos", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 35→
 36→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "Write documentation");
 37→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "Review pull request");
 38→doc.ListInsertString(items, 2, "Deploy to production");
 39→
 40→doc.Commit("Add todo items");
 41→```
 42→
 43→This creates a list: `["Write documentation", "Review pull request", "Deploy to production"]`
 44→
 45→## Numbers
 46→
 47→Insert integer values:
 48→
 49→```csharp
 50→var numbers = doc.PutObject(root, "scores", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 51→
 52→doc.ListInsertInt64(numbers, 0, 100);
 53→doc.ListInsertInt64(numbers, 1, -42);
 54→doc.ListInsertInt64(numbers, 2, 0);
 55→
 56→doc.ListInsertUInt64(numbers, 3, 999);
 57→```
 58→
 59→Use `ListInsertInt64` for signed integers and `ListInsertUInt64` for unsigned integers.
 60→
 61→Insert floating-point values:
 62→
 63→```csharp
 64→var measurements = doc.PutObject(root, "temperatures", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 65→
 66→doc.ListInsertDouble(measurements, 0, 23.5);
 67→doc.ListInsertDouble(measurements, 1, 24.1);
 68→doc.ListInsertDouble(measurements, 2, 22.8);
 69→```
 70→
 71→## Booleans
 72→
 73→Insert true or false values:
 74→
 75→```csharp
 76→var flags = doc.PutObject(root, "completed", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 77→
 78→doc.ListInsertBoolean(flags, 0, true);
 79→doc.ListInsertBoolean(flags, 1, false);
 80→doc.ListInsertBoolean(flags, 2, true);
 81→```
 82→
 83→## Binary Data
 84→
 85→Insert byte arrays:
 86→
 87→```csharp
 88→var hashes = doc.PutObject(root, "checksums", AutomergeContainerType.List);
 89→
 90→var hash1 = new byte[] { 0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, 0xEF };
 91→var hash2 = new byte[] { 0xCA, 0xFE, 0xBA, 0xBE };
 92→
 93→doc.ListInsertBytes(hashes, 0, hash1);
 94→doc.ListInsertBytes(hashes, 1, hash2);
 95→```
 96→
 97→## Null Values
 98→
 99→Insert explicit null values:
100→
101→```csharp
102→var optional = doc.PutObject(root, "optional_values", AutomergeContainerType.List);
103→
104→doc.ListInsertString(optional, 0, "present");
105→doc.ListInsertNull(optional, 1);
106→doc.ListInsertString(optional, 2, "also present");
107→```
108→
109→This creates: `["present", null, "also present"]`
110→
111→## Nested Containers
112→
113→Insert maps, lists, or text containers:
114→
115→```csharp
116→var tasks = doc.PutObject(root, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
117→
118→// Insert a map
119→var task1 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 0, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
120→doc.PutString(task1, "title", "Write tests");
121→doc.PutBoolean(task1, "completed", false);
122→doc.PutInt64(task1, "priority", 1);
123→
124→// Insert another map
125→var task2 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 1, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
126→doc.PutString(task2, "title", "Review code");
127→doc.PutBoolean(task2, "completed", true);
128→doc.PutInt64(task2, "priority", 2);
129→
130→doc.Commit("Add tasks");
131→```
132→
133→This creates:
134→
135→```json
136→{
137→  "tasks": [
138→    {
139→      "title": "Write tests",
140→      "completed": false,
141→      "priority": 1
142→    },
143→    {
144→      "title": "Review code",
145→      "completed": true,
146→      "priority": 2
147→    }
148→  ]
149→}
150→```
151→
152→# Insertion Position
153→
154→The index parameter controls where items appear.
155→
156→## Insert at Beginning
157→
158→Use index 0:
159→
160→```csharp
161→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
162→
163→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");   // ["first"]
164→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "new first"); // ["new first", "first"]
165→```
166→
167→## Insert at End
168→
169→Use the current length as the index:
170→
171→```csharp
172→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");  // ["first"]
173→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "second"); // ["first", "second"]
174→doc.ListInsertString(items, 2, "third");  // ["first", "second", "third"]
175→```
176→
177→## Insert in Middle
178→
179→Use any valid index:
180→
181→```csharp
182→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");  // ["first"]
183→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "third");  // ["first", "third"]
184→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "second"); // ["first", "second", "third"]
185→```
186→
187→# Removing Items
188→
189→Remove items by index using `ListRemove`:
190→
191→```csharp
192→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
193→
194→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "keep this");
195→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "remove this");
196→doc.ListInsertString(items, 2, "keep this too");
197→doc.Commit("Add items");
198→
199→doc.ListRemove(items, 1); // Remove the middle item
200→doc.Commit("Remove item");
201→```
202→
203→After removal, the list contains: `["keep this", "keep this too"]`
204→
205→Indices shift down after removal. Removing index 1 makes the old index 2 become the new index 1.
206→
207→# Getting List Length
208→
209→Check how many items a list contains:
210→
211→```csharp
212→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
213→
214→var length = doc.ListLength(items);
215→Console.WriteLine($"Length: {length}"); // Length: 0
216→
217→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");
218→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "second");
219→
220→length = doc.ListLength(items);
221→Console.WriteLine($"Length: {length}"); // Length: 2
222→```
223→
224→The length changes as you insert and remove items.
225→
226→# Reading List Contents
227→
228→Use `HydrateJson` to get the list as JSON:
229→
230→```csharp
231→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
232→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "apple");
233→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "banana");
234→doc.ListInsertString(items, 2, "cherry");
235→doc.Commit("Add fruits");
236→
237→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
238→using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
239→var itemsArray = parsed.RootElement.GetProperty("items");
240→
241→for (int i = 0; i < itemsArray.GetArrayLength(); i++)
242→{
243→    Console.WriteLine($"Item {i}: {itemsArray[i].GetString()}");
244→}
245→```
246→
247→Output:
248→```
249→Item 0: apple
250→Item 1: banana
251→Item 2: cherry
252→```
253→
254→# Complete Example
255→
256→Here's a task list implementation:
257→
258→```csharp
259→using System;
260→using System.Text.Json;
261→using Ray.Toolkit.Automerge;
262→
263→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
264→var root = doc.RootId;
265→
266→// Create a task list
267→var tasks = doc.PutObject(root, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
268→
269→// Add first task
270→var task1 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 0, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
271→doc.PutString(task1, "id", "TASK-001");
272→doc.PutString(task1, "title", "Design database schema");
273→doc.PutBoolean(task1, "completed", false);
274→doc.PutInt64(task1, "priority", 1);
275→doc.PutTimestamp(task1, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
276→
277→// Add task tags as nested list
278→var task1Tags = doc.PutObject(task1, "tags", AutomergeContainerType.List);
279→doc.ListInsertString(task1Tags, 0, "database");
280→doc.ListInsertString(task1Tags, 1, "backend");
281→
282→// Add second task
283→var task2 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 1, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
284→doc.PutString(task2, "id", "TASK-002");
285→doc.PutString(task2, "title", "Create API endpoints");
286→doc.PutBoolean(task2, "completed", false);
287→doc.PutInt64(task2, "priority", 2);
288→doc.PutTimestamp(task2, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
289→
290→var task2Tags = doc.PutObject(task2, "tags", AutomergeContainerType.List);
291→doc.ListInsertString(task2Tags, 0, "api");
292→doc.ListInsertString(task2Tags, 1, "backend");
293→
294→// Add third task
295→var task3 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 2, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
296→doc.PutString(task3, "id", "TASK-003");
297→doc.PutString(task3, "title", "Write unit tests");
298→doc.PutBoolean(task3, "completed", true);
299→doc.PutInt64(task3, "priority", 3);
300→doc.PutTimestamp(task3, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
301→
302→var task3Tags = doc.PutObject(task3, "tags", AutomergeContainerType.List);
303→doc.ListInsertString(task3Tags, 0, "testing");
304→doc.ListInsertString(task3Tags, 1, "quality");
305→
306→doc.Commit("Create task list");
307→
308→Console.WriteLine($"Task count: {doc.ListLength(tasks)}");
309→
310→// Display tasks
311→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
312→using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
313→var tasksArray = parsed.RootElement.GetProperty("tasks");
314→
315→for (int i = 0; i < tasksArray.GetArrayLength(); i++)
316→{
317→    var task = tasksArray[i];
318→    var title = task.GetProperty("title").GetString();
319→    var completed = task.GetProperty("completed").GetBoolean();
320→    var priority = task.GetProperty("priority").GetInt64();
321→
322→    var status = completed ? "✓" : " ";
323→    Console.WriteLine($"[{status}] Priority {priority}: {title}");
324→
325→    if (task.TryGetProperty("tags", out var tags))
326→    {
327→        Console.Write("    Tags: ");
328→        for (int j = 0; j < tags.GetArrayLength(); j++)
329→        {
330→            Console.Write(tags[j].GetString());
331→            if (j < tags.GetArrayLength() - 1) Console.Write(", ");
332→        }
333→        Console.WriteLine();
334→    }
335→}
336→
337→// Mark first task as completed
338→var updatedTask1 = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 0, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
339→doc.PutBoolean(updatedTask1, "completed", true);
340→doc.Commit("Complete first task");
341→```
342→
343→# Merge Behavior
344→
345→Lists merge concurrent insertions intelligently.
346→
347→## Different Positions
348→
349→When users insert at different positions, both insertions appear:
350→
351→```csharp
352→// Alice's document
353→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
354→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
355→var aliceList = alice.PutObject(aliceRoot, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
356→alice.ListInsertString(aliceList, 0, "Alice's item at 0");
357→alice.Commit("Alice: Insert at 0");
358→
359→// Bob's document
360→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
361→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
362→var bobList = bob.PutObject(bobRoot, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
363→bob.ListInsertString(bobList, 0, "Bob's item at 0");
364→bob.Commit("Bob: Insert at 0");
365→
366→// After merge, both items appear
367→var bobChanges = bob.GetChanges(new string[0]);
368→alice.ApplyChanges(bobChanges);
369→
370→// Result: Both items present, order determined by Automerge's conflict resolution
371→```
372→
373→## Same Position
374→
375→When both users insert at the same position, Automerge preserves both items:
376→
377→```csharp
378→// Start with a shared list: ["first", "third"]
379→var aliceList = alice.PutObject(aliceRoot, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
380→alice.ListInsertString(aliceList, 0, "first");
381→alice.ListInsertString(aliceList, 1, "third");
382→alice.Commit("Alice: Add first and third");
383→
384→// Bob gets Alice's state
385→var aliceChanges = alice.GetChanges(new string[0]);
386→var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
387→bob.ApplyChanges(aliceChanges);
388→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
389→var bobList = bob.PutObject(bobRoot, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
390→
391→// Both insert "second" at index 1
392→alice.ListInsertString(aliceList, 1, "Alice's second");
393→alice.Commit("Alice: Insert at 1");
394→
395→bob.ListInsertString(bobList, 1, "Bob's second");
396→bob.Commit("Bob: Insert at 1");
397→
398→// After merge, both insertions appear
399→// Automerge determines a consistent order across all peers
400→```
401→
402→## Concurrent Removals
403→
404→If two users remove different items, both removals apply:
405→
406→```csharp
407→// Start with ["a", "b", "c"]
408→alice.ListRemove(aliceList, 0); // Remove "a"
409→bob.ListRemove(bobList, 2);     // Remove "c"
410→
411→// After merge: ["b"]
412→```
413→
414→If both remove the same item, the removal applies once:
415→
416→```csharp
417→// Both remove index 1
418→alice.ListRemove(aliceList, 1);
419→bob.ListRemove(bobList, 1);
420→
421→// After merge: Item removed once, not twice
422→```
423→
424→# Best Practices
425→
426→## Use Lists for Ordered Data
427→
428→Choose lists when order matters:
429→
430→```csharp
431→// Good: Ordered steps
432→var steps = doc.PutObject(root, "steps", AutomergeContainerType.List);
433→doc.ListInsertString(steps, 0, "Step 1: Initialize");
434→doc.ListInsertString(steps, 1, "Step 2: Configure");
435→doc.ListInsertString(steps, 2, "Step 3: Deploy");
436→```
437→
438→For unordered data, consider maps instead:
439→
440→```csharp
441→// Better for unordered data: Use a map with IDs as keys
442→var users = doc.PutObject(root, "users", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
443→var user1 = doc.PutObject(users, "user_123", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
444→doc.PutString(user1, "name", "Alice");
445→```
446→
447→## Use Nested Maps in Lists
448→
449→Store structured items as nested maps:
450→
451→```csharp
452→// Good: Structured items
453→var tasks = doc.PutObject(root, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
454→var task = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 0, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
455→doc.PutString(task, "title", "Task title");
456→doc.PutBoolean(task, "completed", false);
457→doc.PutInt64(task, "priority", 1);
458→
459→// Less flexible: Multiple parallel lists
460→var titles = doc.PutObject(root, "task_titles", AutomergeContainerType.List);
461→var completed = doc.PutObject(root, "task_completed", AutomergeContainerType.List);
462→var priorities = doc.PutObject(root, "task_priorities", AutomergeContainerType.List);
463→```
464→
465→## Avoid Large Lists
466→
467→Very large lists (thousands of items) can impact performance. Consider:
468→
469→- Pagination: Load and display subsets
470→- Filtering: Store only relevant items
471→- Splitting: Use multiple documents for large datasets
472→
473→## Use Stable IDs
474→
475→Store unique identifiers in list items to track them across changes:
476→
477→```csharp
478→var tasks = doc.PutObject(root, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
479→var task = doc.ListInsertObject(tasks, 0, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
480→doc.PutString(task, "id", "TASK-001"); // Stable identifier
481→doc.PutString(task, "title", "Do something");
482→```
483→
484→This helps you find specific items even after the list order changes.
485→
486→## Insert in Logical Order
487→
488→Insert items in a natural order when possible:
489→
490→```csharp
491→// Good: Append at the end
492→var items = doc.PutObject(root, "items", AutomergeContainerType.List);
493→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");
494→doc.ListInsertString(items, 1, "second");  // Append after first
495→doc.ListInsertString(items, 2, "third");   // Append after second
496→
497→// Works but less predictable: Insert at beginning repeatedly
498→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "third");
499→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "second");
500→doc.ListInsertString(items, 0, "first");
501→```
