  2→title: Synchronization
  3→description: Learn how to sync Automerge documents between multiple peers using the sync protocol
  4→---
  5→
  6→# Overview
  7→
  8→Automerge uses a sync protocol to exchange changes between documents efficiently.
  9→
 10→The protocol minimizes network traffic by sending only the differences between documents. It handles network interruptions gracefully and works with any transport layer.
 11→
 12→# Sync Protocol Basics
 13→
 14→The sync protocol follows a request-response pattern. Two peers exchange sync messages until both have all changes.
 15→
 16→## How It Works
 17→
 18→Each peer maintains a sync state tracking what the other peer knows. The protocol exchanges messages in rounds:
 19→
 20→1. Peer A generates a sync message based on what Peer B knows
 21→2. Peer A sends the message to Peer B
 22→3. Peer B receives the message and updates its document
 23→4. Peer B generates a response message
 24→5. Peer B sends its message to Peer A
 25→6. Repeat until both peers have no more messages to send
 26→
 27→# Creating Sync State
 28→
 29→Each document needs a sync state for each peer it syncs with:
 30→
 31→```csharp
 32→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 33→using var syncState = doc.CreateSyncState();
 34→```
 35→
 36→Sync states are disposable resources. Use `using` statements for proper cleanup.
 37→
 38→# Basic Synchronization
 39→
 40→Here's a complete sync between two documents:
 41→
 42→```csharp
 43→using Ray.Toolkit.Automerge;
 44→
 45→// Alice creates a document
 46→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 47→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
 48→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "title", "Project Plan");
 49→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "owner", "Alice");
 50→alice.Commit("Alice: Initial draft");
 51→
 52→// Bob creates a document
 53→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 54→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
 55→bob.PutString(bobRoot, "status", "draft");
 56→bob.PutString(bobRoot, "priority", "high");
 57→bob.Commit("Bob: Add metadata");
 58→
 59→// Create sync states
 60→using var aliceSyncState = alice.CreateSyncState();
 61→using var bobSyncState = bob.CreateSyncState();
 62→
 63→// Sync loop
 64→bool hasMoreMessages = true;
 65→int roundCount = 0;
 66→int maxRounds = 10;
 67→
 68→while (hasMoreMessages && roundCount < maxRounds)
 69→{
 70→    hasMoreMessages = false;
 71→
 72→    // Alice generates message for Bob
 73→    var aliceMessage = alice.GenerateSyncMessage(aliceSyncState);
 74→    if (aliceMessage != null)
 75→    {
 76→        bob.ReceiveSyncMessage(bobSyncState, aliceMessage);
 77→        hasMoreMessages = true;
 78→    }
 79→
 80→    // Bob generates message for Alice
 81→    var bobMessage = bob.GenerateSyncMessage(bobSyncState);
 82→    if (bobMessage != null)
 83→    {
 84→        alice.ReceiveSyncMessage(aliceSyncState, bobMessage);
 85→        hasMoreMessages = true;
 86→    }
 87→
 88→    roundCount++;
 89→}
 90→
 91→// Both documents now contain all changes
 92→var aliceJson = alice.HydrateJson();
 93→var bobJson = bob.HydrateJson();
 94→// Both have: title, owner, status, priority
 95→```
 96→
 97→# Generating Sync Messages
 98→
 99→Generate messages using `GenerateSyncMessage`:
100→
101→```csharp
102→var message = doc.GenerateSyncMessage(syncState);
103→
104→if (message == null)
105→{
106→    Console.WriteLine("No changes to send");
107→}
108→else
109→{
110→    Console.WriteLine($"Message size: {message.Length} bytes");
111→    // Send message to peer
112→}
113→```
114→
115→The method returns `null` when there's nothing more to send. This signals the sync is complete.
116→
117→# Receiving Sync Messages
118→
119→Receive messages using `ReceiveSyncMessage`:
120→
121→```csharp
122→// Receive message from peer
123→byte[] incomingMessage = GetMessageFromPeer();
124→
125→if (incomingMessage != null && incomingMessage.Length > 0)
126→{
127→    doc.ReceiveSyncMessage(syncState, incomingMessage);
128→    Console.WriteLine("Applied changes from peer");
129→}
130→```
131→
132→This updates the document with changes from the peer.
133→
134→# Sync Helper Function
135→
136→Here's a reusable function for syncing two documents:
137→
138→```csharp
139→void SyncDocuments(
140→    AutomergeDocument doc1,
141→    AutomergeDocument doc2,
142→    AutomergeSyncState state1,
143→    AutomergeSyncState state2,
144→    int maxRounds = 10)
145→{
146→    for (int i = 0; i < maxRounds; i++)
147→    {
148→        var msg1 = doc1.GenerateSyncMessage(state1);
149→        var msg2 = doc2.GenerateSyncMessage(state2);
150→
151→        if (msg1 == null && msg2 == null)
152→        {
153→            // Both sides have nothing to send; sync complete
154→            break;
155→        }
156→
157→        if (msg1 != null)
158→        {
159→            doc2.ReceiveSyncMessage(state2, msg1);
160→        }
161→
162→        if (msg2 != null)
163→        {
164→            doc1.ReceiveSyncMessage(state1, msg2);
165→        }
166→    }
167→}
168→
169→// Usage
170→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
171→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
172→using var aliceSync = alice.CreateSyncState();
173→using var bobSync = bob.CreateSyncState();
174→
175→// ... make changes to documents ...
176→
177→SyncDocuments(alice, bob, aliceSync, bobSync);
178→```
179→
180→# Multi-Peer Synchronization
181→
182→Sync with multiple peers by maintaining separate sync states:
183→
184→```csharp
185→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
186→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
187→using var carol = AutomergeDocument.Create();
188→
189→// Alice needs sync states for both Bob and Carol
190→using var aliceToBob = alice.CreateSyncState();
191→using var aliceToCarol = alice.CreateSyncState();
192→
193→// Bob needs sync states for Alice and Carol
194→using var bobToAlice = bob.CreateSyncState();
195→using var bobToCarol = bob.CreateSyncState();
196→
197→// Carol needs sync states for Alice and Bob
198→using var carolToAlice = carol.CreateSyncState();
199→using var carolToBob = carol.CreateSyncState();
200→
201→// Sync Alice with Bob
202→SyncDocuments(alice, bob, aliceToBob, bobToAlice);
203→
204→// Sync Alice with Carol
205→SyncDocuments(alice, carol, aliceToCarol, carolToAlice);
206→
207→// Sync Bob with Carol
208→SyncDocuments(bob, carol, bobToCarol, carolToBob);
209→
210→// All three documents now have all changes
211→```
212→
213→# Incremental Synchronization
214→
215→The sync protocol is incremental. After the initial sync, subsequent syncs only send new changes:
216→
217→```csharp
218→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
219→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
220→using var aliceSync = alice.CreateSyncState();
221→using var bobSync = bob.CreateSyncState();
222→
223→// Initial sync
224→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
225→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "version", "1.0");
226→alice.Commit("v1.0");
227→
228→SyncDocuments(alice, bob, aliceSync, bobSync);
229→// Bob now has version 1.0
230→
231→// Alice makes more changes
232→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "version", "1.1");
233→alice.Commit("v1.1");
234→
235→// Sync again - only new changes transfer
236→SyncDocuments(alice, bob, aliceSync, bobSync);
237→// Bob now has version 1.1
238→
239→// Very efficient for subsequent syncs
240→```
241→
242→The sync state remembers what each peer knows, so only deltas transfer.
243→
244→# Network Transport
245→
246→The sync protocol is transport-agnostic. You can use it over:
247→
248→**WebSockets**
249→```csharp
250→// Pseudo-code for WebSocket transport
251→websocket.OnMessage += (sender, e) =>
252→{
253→    var message = e.Data;
254→    doc.ReceiveSyncMessage(syncState, message);
255→
256→    var response = doc.GenerateSyncMessage(syncState);
257→    if (response != null)
258→    {
259→        websocket.Send(response);
260→    }
261→};
262→```
263→
264→**HTTP**
265→```csharp
266→// Pseudo-code for HTTP transport
267→var message = doc.GenerateSyncMessage(syncState);
268→if (message != null)
269→{
270→    var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/sync", new ByteArrayContent(message));
271→    var responseBytes = await response.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync();
272→    doc.ReceiveSyncMessage(syncState, responseBytes);
273→}
274→```
275→
276→**gRPC, QUIC, or any binary protocol**
277→
278→The sync messages are just byte arrays. Transport them however you need.
279→
280→# Persisting Sync State
281→
282→Sync states can be persisted between sessions:
283→
284→```csharp
285→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
286→using var syncState = doc.CreateSyncState();
287→
288→// Sync with peer...
289→
290→// Save sync state
291→var encoded = syncState.Encode();
292→File.WriteAllBytes("sync-state.bin", encoded);
293→
294→// Later: Restore sync state
295→var loadedBytes = File.ReadAllBytes("sync-state.bin");
296→using var restoredState = AutomergeSyncState.Decode(loadedBytes);
297→
298→// Continue syncing with the restored state
299→var message = doc.GenerateSyncMessage(restoredState);
300→```
301→
302→This lets you resume syncs after application restarts without re-sending all changes.
303→
304→# Resetting Sync State
305→
306→Sometimes you need to reset sync state and start fresh:
307→
308→```csharp
309→using var syncState = doc.CreateSyncState();
310→
311→// Use the sync state...
312→
313→// Reset to initial state
314→syncState.Reset();
315→
316→// Next sync will send all changes again
317→var message = doc.GenerateSyncMessage(syncState);
318→```
319→
320→Reset when you want to force a full resync or when the peer's state is unknown.
321→
322→# Checking Sync Progress
323→
324→Query shared heads to see what the peer has acknowledged:
325→
326→```csharp
327→using var syncState = doc.CreateSyncState();
328→
329→// Sync with peer...
330→
331→var sharedHeads = syncState.SharedHeads;
332→Console.WriteLine($"Peer has acknowledged {sharedHeads.Count} heads:");
333→foreach (var head in sharedHeads)
334→{
335→    Console.WriteLine($"  {head}");
336→}
337→```
338→
339→Shared heads represent the commits both peers agree they have.
340→
341→# Complete Example
342→
343→Here's a comprehensive sync example with three peers:
344→
345→```csharp
346→using System;
347→using System.Text.Json;
348→using Ray.Toolkit.Automerge;
349→
350→// Create three team members' documents
351→using var alice = AutomergeDocument.Create();
352→using var bob = AutomergeDocument.Create();
353→using var carol = AutomergeDocument.Create();
354→
355→// Alice creates initial project structure
356→var aliceRoot = alice.RootId;
357→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "project_name", "Website Redesign");
358→alice.PutString(aliceRoot, "owner", "Alice");
359→alice.Commit("Alice: Create project");
360→
361→Console.WriteLine("Alice created the project");
362→
363→// Bob works offline and adds tasks
364→var bobRoot = bob.RootId;
365→bob.PutString(bobRoot, "status", "in-progress");
366→var tasks = bob.PutObject(bobRoot, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
367→bob.ListInsertString(tasks, 0, "Design mockups");
368→bob.Commit("Bob: Add tasks");
369→
370→Console.WriteLine("Bob added tasks (offline)");
371→
372→// Carol works offline and adds metadata
373→var carolRoot = carol.RootId;
374→carol.PutString(carolRoot, "priority", "high");
375→carol.PutTimestamp(carolRoot, "deadline", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddDays(30).ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
376→carol.Commit("Carol: Add metadata");
377→
378→Console.WriteLine("Carol added metadata (offline)");
379→
380→// Now they come online and sync
381→
382→// Alice syncs with Bob
383→using var aliceToBobSync = alice.CreateSyncState();
384→using var bobToAliceSync = bob.CreateSyncState();
385→
386→Console.WriteLine("\nSyncing Alice ↔ Bob...");
387→SyncDocuments(alice, bob, aliceToBobSync, bobToAliceSync);
388→Console.WriteLine("Alice and Bob synced");
389→
390→// Alice syncs with Carol
391→using var aliceToCarolSync = alice.CreateSyncState();
392→using var carolToAliceSync = carol.CreateSyncState();
393→
394→Console.WriteLine("\nSyncing Alice ↔ Carol...");
395→SyncDocuments(alice, carol, aliceToCarolSync, carolToAliceSync);
396→Console.WriteLine("Alice and Carol synced");
397→
398→// Bob syncs with Carol
399→using var bobToCarolSync = bob.CreateSyncState();
400→using var carolToBobSync = carol.CreateSyncState();
401→
402→Console.WriteLine("\nSyncing Bob ↔ Carol...");
403→SyncDocuments(bob, carol, bobToCarolSync, carolToBobSync);
404→Console.WriteLine("Bob and Carol synced");
405→
406→// Verify convergence - all three should have identical data
407→var aliceJson = alice.HydrateJson();
408→var bobJson = bob.HydrateJson();
409→var carolJson = carol.HydrateJson();
410→
411→Console.WriteLine("\nFinal state:");
412→Console.WriteLine("Alice's document:");
413→DisplayJson(aliceJson);
414→
415→Console.WriteLine("\nBob's document:");
416→DisplayJson(bobJson);
417→
418→Console.WriteLine("\nCarol's document:");
419→DisplayJson(carolJson);
420→
421→void SyncDocuments(
422→    AutomergeDocument doc1,
423→    AutomergeDocument doc2,
424→    AutomergeSyncState state1,
425→    AutomergeSyncState state2,
426→    int maxRounds = 10)
427→{
428→    for (int i = 0; i < maxRounds; i++)
429→    {
430→        var msg1 = doc1.GenerateSyncMessage(state1);
431→        var msg2 = doc2.GenerateSyncMessage(state2);
432→
433→        if (msg1 == null && msg2 == null)
434→        {
435→            break;
436→        }
437→
438→        if (msg1 != null)
439→        {
440→            doc2.ReceiveSyncMessage(state2, msg1);
441→        }
442→
443→        if (msg2 != null)
444→        {
445→            doc1.ReceiveSyncMessage(state1, msg2);
446→        }
447→    }
448→}
449→
450→void DisplayJson(string json)
451→{
452→    using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
453→    Console.WriteLine(JsonSerializer.Serialize(parsed.RootElement, new JsonSerializerOptions
454→    {
455→        WriteIndented = true
456→    }));
457→}
458→```
